  </div>
  <div class="topbar-progress-wrap"><div class="topbar-bar-bg"><div class="topbar-bar-fill" id="globalFill" style="width:0%"></div></div><div class="topbar-count" id="globalCount">0 / 0</div></div>
  <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--dim);font-family:var(--mono)">Module 07</div>
</div>
<nav class="section-nav" aria-label="Jump to section"><div class="section-nav-inner" id="sectionNav"></div></nav>
<div class="hero">
  <img src="https://risebirdportal.blob.core.windows.net/logo/risebird.png" alt="RiseBird">
  <div class="hero-eyebrow">Module 07 · Contradiction-Safe Evaluations</div>
  <h1 class="hero-title">AI Contradiction<br>Analysis Agent</h1>
  <p class="hero-sub">How to write interview evaluations that stay internally consistent, justify your judgment, and avoid unnecessary contradiction penalties from the AI Contradiction Analysis Agent — without softening your scoring.</p>
  <div class="hero-badges">
    <span class="badge"><span class="badge-dot" style="background:var(--green)"></span>Activates suppression rules</span>
    <span class="badge"><span class="badge-dot" style="background:var(--amber)"></span>Common pitfall</span>
    <span class="badge"><span class="badge-dot" style="background:var(--red)"></span>Triggers true contradiction</span>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="key-callout-wrap"><div class="key-callout"><div class="key-callout-icon">⚖️</div><div><div class="key-callout-title">The agent doesn't punish strict interviewers — it punishes unexplained ones</div><div class="key-callout-body">Disagreeing with the AI is allowed. <strong>Strict, harsh, even rejecting partially-correct candidates</strong> — all allowed. The contradiction engine only penalizes <strong>material, unexplained disagreement</strong>. Your comments are what convert disagreement into <em style="color:var(--green);font-style:normal">Valid Disagreement</em> instead of <em style="color:var(--red);font-style:normal">True Contradiction</em>.</div></div></div></div>
<main class="main" id="main">
<section class="section" id="sec-principle"><div class="section-header"><div class="section-icon">01</div><div class="section-meta"><div class="section-num">Foundation</div><div class="section-title">The Core Principle</div><div class="section-subtitle">The AI is not ground truth — your judgment is. The job is to make your reasoning legible.</div></div></div>
<p>The contradiction system is explicit:</p>
<div class="callout tip"><div class="callout-icon">💡</div><div class="callout-body"><strong>Rule</strong> AI–human difference alone is never enough for a High or Critical contradiction. Strict scoring, harsher evaluations, and rejecting partially-correct candidates are all allowed — provided your reasoning is documented.</div></div>
<p>The biggest risk is <strong>not</strong> being stricter than the AI. The biggest risks are:</p>
<ul><li>Weak or vague justification</li><li>Score, status, and comment that don't agree with each other</li><li>Unsupported rejection or selection</li><li>Missing reasoning behind a harsh score</li></ul>
<p>You are safe when your comments justify your scores, your status aligns with your observations, your concerns are clearly documented, and your evaluation logic is internally coherent.</p>
</section>
<section class="section" id="sec-checks"><div class="section-header"><div class="section-icon">02</div><div class="section-meta"><div class="section-num">What Gets Audited</div><div class="section-title">The Six Parameters the Agent Evaluates</div><div class="section-subtitle">Every interview is checked against six fields. One of them carries most of the weight.</div></div></div>
<table class="param-table"><thead><tr><th style="width:38%">Parameter</th><th>What the agent checks</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td class="param-name">TechSkillScore</td><td>Does the technical score match the observed performance?</td></tr>
<tr><td class="param-name">ScoreRubricScore</td><td>Does the rubric score logically align with the evaluation?</td></tr>
<tr><td class="param-name">QuestionDiscussedRating</td><td>Were the answers meaningfully discussed and understood?</td></tr>
<tr><td class="param-name">CommunicationScore</td><td>Was communication clarity consistent with the rest of the evaluation?</td></tr>
<tr><td class="param-name">InterviewStatus</td><td>Does the final decision logically match the evidence?</td></tr>
<tr class="param-most"><td class="param-name">InterviewerComment</td><td>Does the written comment justify the evaluation? <strong>This field activates contradiction-suppression logic across every other parameter.</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="callout warn"><div class="callout-icon">⚠️</div><div class="callout-body"><strong>Why InterviewerComment matters most</strong> A strong comment can suppress contradiction across multiple parameters at once. A weak comment leaves every other field exposed — even when the scores themselves are reasonable.</div></div>
</section>
<section class="section" id="sec-golden"><div class="section-header"><div class="section-icon">03</div><div class="section-meta"><div class="section-num">The Golden Rule</div><div class="section-title">Every score must be explainable from your comments</div><div class="section-subtitle">If the comment supports the scores and the status, contradiction collapses into valid disagreement.</div></div></div>
<p>The contradiction engine performs three internal checks on every evaluation:</p>
<ul><li><strong>HumanDecisionJustified</strong> — is the decision supported by the written comment?</li><li><strong>Internal consistency</strong> — do score, status, and comment tell the same story?</li><li><strong>Status justification</strong> — does the final status follow from the evidence?</li></ul>
<p>When all three pass, AI disagreement is reclassified:</p>
<div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:18px 0 6px"><span class="result-chip contra">True Contradiction</span><span style="color:var(--dim);font-family:var(--mono);font-size:13px">→ becomes →</span><span class="result-chip valid">Valid Disagreement</span></div>
</section>
<section class="section" id="sec-fundamentals"><div class="section-header"><div class="section-icon">04</div><div class="section-meta"><div class="section-num">Suppression Trigger #1</div><div class="section-title">Document Fundamentals Failure Clearly</div><div class="section-subtitle">Naming weak fundamentals is the single strongest protective signal in your comment.</div></div></div>
<p>The agent strongly protects interviewers when comments mention weak fundamentals, failed basics, shallow understanding, inability to explain core concepts, lack of reasoning depth, memorized answers, or incomplete debugging ability. These activate:</p>
<span class="trigger">FundamentalsFailure = true</span>
<p style="margin-top:12px">…which reduces contradiction penalties on TechSkillScore, RubricScore, and InterviewStatus simultaneously.</p>
<div class="compare"><div class="compare-card good"><div class="compare-label"><span class="compare-label-dot"></span>Good — specific, observable</div><div class="compare-list"><div class="ex">Candidate answered partially but failed to explain core concepts.</div><div class="ex">Knew syntax-level answers but lacked depth in reasoning.</div><div class="ex">Unable to explain time-complexity tradeoffs clearly.</div><div class="ex">Could solve direct problems but fundamentals were weak.</div><div class="ex">Debugging ability was below expected level.</div><div class="ex">Struggled with follow-up conceptual questions.</div></div></div><div class="compare-card bad"><div class="compare-label"><span class="compare-label-dot"></span>Bad — vague, unjustified</div><div class="compare-list"><div class="ex">Not good.</div><div class="ex">Weak.</div><div class="ex">Poor performance.</div><div class="ex">Average candidate.</div></div></div></div>
</section>
<section class="section" id="sec-proxy"><div class="section-header"><div class="section-icon">05</div><div class="section-meta"><div class="section-num">Suppression Trigger #2</div><div class="section-title">Surface Authenticity &amp; Proxy Concerns</div><div class="section-subtitle">If you suspect external assistance or proxy participation, name it concretely — vague suspicion doesn't trigger the override.</div></div></div>
<p>Mentioning concrete authenticity signals — copied responses, external assistance, suspicious pauses, non-independent answering, prompted responses, inconsistent confidence — activates:</p>
<span class="trigger">ProxyOverride = true</span>
<p style="margin-top:12px">…which strongly reduces contradiction across <span class="param-name" style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--blue-light)">TechSkillScore</span>, <span class="param-name" style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--blue-light)">RubricScore</span>, and <span class="param-name" style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--blue-light)">InterviewStatus</span>.</p>
<div class="compare"><div class="compare-card good"><div class="compare-label"><span class="compare-label-dot"></span>Good — observable signals</div><div class="compare-list"><div class="ex">Responses appeared externally assisted.</div><div class="ex">Candidate showed signs of non-independent answering.</div><div class="ex">Strong suspicion of proxy participation.</div><div class="ex">Answers lacked natural reasoning continuity.</div><div class="ex">Struggled heavily in follow-up probing despite initial correct answers.</div><div class="ex">Response pattern appeared memorized rather than understood.</div></div></div><div class="compare-card bad"><div class="compare-label"><span class="compare-label-dot"></span>Bad — vague, unactionable</div><div class="compare-list"><div class="ex">Maybe proxy.</div><div class="ex">Suspicious.</div><div class="ex">Not confident.</div></div></div></div>
</section>
<section class="section" id="sec-partial"><div class="section-header"><div class="section-icon">06</div><div class="section-meta"><div class="section-num">Suppression Trigger #3</div><div class="section-title">Explain Why Partial Correctness Wasn't Enough</div><div class="section-subtitle">The AI tends to score working-but-shallow answers positively. Document why you didn't.</div></div></div>
<p>When the candidate produced partially-correct answers, working code, or technically acceptable output but you scored them low, the comment must spell out the gap: shallow reasoning, weak explanation depth, inability to defend the solution, missing optimization understanding, conceptual weakness.</p>
<div class="compare-card good" style="margin:16px 0 22px"><div class="compare-label"><span class="compare-label-dot"></span>Good — converts disagreement to valid</div><div class="compare-list"><div class="ex">Candidate produced a working solution but could not explain optimization tradeoffs.</div><div class="ex">Answers were directionally correct but reasoning depth was insufficient.</div><div class="ex">Candidate relied on memorized patterns and struggled during probing.</div><div class="ex">Implementation was acceptable but conceptual understanding remained weak.</div></div></div>
<div class="callout success"><div class="callout-icon">✓</div><div class="callout-body"><strong>Result</strong> Disagreement is reclassified as <em style="font-style:normal;color:var(--green)">ValidDisagreement</em> instead of contradiction.</div></div>
</section>
<section class="section" id="sec-consistency"><div class="section-header"><div class="section-icon">07</div><div class="section-meta"><div class="section-num">Internal Consistency</div><div class="section-title">Score, Status, and Comment Must Tell One Story</div><div class="section-subtitle">The agent compares each pair: score↔comment, status↔comment, score↔status. Mismatch is the easiest way to spike contradiction.</div></div></div>
<div class="eval-card bad"><div class="eval-header">⊘ Inconsistent — high contradiction risk</div><div class="eval-row"><div class="eval-key">TechSkillScore</div><div class="eval-val">3 / 10</div></div><div class="eval-row"><div class="eval-key">InterviewStatus</div><div class="eval-val">Select</div></div><div class="eval-row"><div class="eval-key">InterviewerComment</div><div class="eval-val">"Good technical understanding."</div></div></div>
<div class="eval-card good"><div class="eval-header">✓ Internally coherent — contradiction suppressed</div><div class="eval-row"><div class="eval-key">TechSkillScore</div><div class="eval-val">3 / 10</div></div><div class="eval-row"><div class="eval-key">InterviewStatus</div><div class="eval-val">Reject</div></div><div class="eval-row"><div class="eval-key">InterviewerComment</div><div class="eval-val">"Candidate struggled with fundamentals, debugging, and reasoning depth despite partial syntax correctness."</div></div></div>
</section>
<section class="section" id="sec-decisions"><div class="section-header"><div class="section-icon">08</div><div class="section-meta"><div class="section-num">Decision Patterns</div><div class="section-title">Safe Patterns for Reject &amp; Borderline Calls</div><div class="section-subtitle">Two situations carry the most contradiction risk. Both are easily defused with structured language.</div></div></div>
<h3>Reject decisions</h3>
<p>Strong reject comments cover at least three of: fundamentals gap, weak reasoning, inability to explain, shallow understanding, debugging weakness, inconsistent performance, non-independent answering, lack of confidence under probing.</p>
<div class="compare-card good" style="margin:12px 0 22px"><div class="compare-label"><span class="compare-label-dot"></span>Strong reject — full coverage</div><div class="compare-list"><div class="ex">Candidate could answer direct questions superficially but failed deeper conceptual probing. Fundamentals in data structures and complexity analysis were weak. Debugging ability was below expectations and reasoning lacked clarity. Overall performance did not meet required technical depth.</div></div></div>
<h3>Borderline decisions</h3>
<p>Borderline calls carry their own contradiction risk because the agent has more room to disagree. Explicitly call out the mixed performance — partial correctness, varied depth across topics, weaker optimization discussion.</p>
<div class="compare-card good" style="margin:12px 0 8px"><div class="compare-label"><span class="compare-label-dot"></span>Strong borderline — explains the mixed read</div><div class="compare-list"><div class="ex">Candidate demonstrated partial problem-solving capability and acceptable coding structure, but depth of understanding varied across topics. Follow-up reasoning and optimization discussion were weaker than expected. Performance falls into borderline consideration.</div></div></div>
</section>
<section class="section" id="sec-comm"><div class="section-header"><div class="section-icon">09</div><div class="section-meta"><div class="section-num">CommunicationScore</div><div class="section-title">Avoid Direct Opposites — Use Calibrated Language</div><div class="section-subtitle">Minor strictness differences on communication aren't penalized. Direct contradictions with the AI are.</div></div></div>
<p>The contradiction engine doesn't penalize small differences in communication scoring. It does penalize <strong>directly opposite descriptions</strong> — for example, the AI calls the candidate clear and confident while you call them incoherent.</p>
<div class="compare"><div class="compare-card bad"><div class="compare-label"><span class="compare-label-dot"></span>High risk — direct opposite</div><div class="compare-list"><div class="ex">Communication was poor and incoherent.</div></div><p style="font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);margin:10px 0 0;font-style:italic">…when the AI describes the candidate as communicating clearly and confidently.</p></div><div class="compare-card good"><div class="compare-label"><span class="compare-label-dot"></span>Safer — explainable nuance</div><div class="compare-list"><div class="ex">Candidate was able to communicate answers but explanations lacked structure and depth during probing.</div></div><p style="font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);margin:10px 0 0;font-style:italic">Same low score, but the disagreement is now explainable.</p></div></div>
</section>
<section class="section" id="sec-structure"><div class="section-header"><div class="section-icon">10</div><div class="section-meta"><div class="section-num">Comment Structure</div><div class="section-title">A Reusable Four-Part Template</div><div class="section-subtitle">Use this structure consistently. It guarantees you've covered every input the suppression logic looks for.</div></div></div>
<div class="template-block"><div class="tpl-step"><div class="tpl-step-num">PART 01</div><div class="tpl-step-title">Technical Capability</div><div class="tpl-step-list"><span>· Depth &amp; correctness</span><span>· Fundamentals</span><span>· Debugging ability</span><span>· Reasoning quality</span></div></div><div class="tpl-step"><div class="tpl-step-num">PART 02</div><div class="tpl-step-title">Communication / Explanation</div><div class="tpl-step-list"><span>· Clarity</span><span>· Confidence</span><span>· Structured explanation</span><span>· Quality under probing</span></div></div><div class="tpl-step"><div class="tpl-step-num">PART 03</div><div class="tpl-step-title">Behavioral / Authenticity</div><div class="tpl-step-list"><span>· Independence</span><span>· Consistency across topics</span><span>· Suspicious patterns</span><span>· Confidence mismatch</span></div></div><div class="tpl-step"><div class="tpl-step-num">PART 04</div><div class="tpl-step-title">Final Recommendation Logic</div><div class="tpl-step-list"><span>· Why select / reject / borderline</span><span>· Which signals carried the most weight</span></div></div></div>
<h3>A model comment that hits all four</h3>
<div class="compare-card good" style="margin:8px 0 0"><div class="compare-label"><span class="compare-label-dot"></span>Strong &amp; safe</div><div class="compare-list"><div class="ex">Candidate demonstrated partial technical capability with acceptable implementation of direct problems. However, understanding of core concepts and optimization tradeoffs was weak during follow-up discussion. Debugging and reasoning depth were below expectations. Communication was understandable but lacked structured explanation under probing. Candidate also showed inconsistent confidence across questions, raising concerns about independent problem solving. Based on overall technical depth and fundamentals, performance does not meet the required bar.</div></div></div>
<p style="margin-top:14px;font-size:14px;color:var(--dim)">Internally consistent · explains the harsh score · justifies reject · activates suppression logic · minimizes contradiction risk.</p>
</section>
<section class="section" id="sec-mistakes"><div class="section-header"><div class="section-icon">11</div><div class="section-meta"><div class="section-num">High-Risk Mistakes</div><div class="section-title">Five Patterns That Spike Contradiction</div><div class="section-subtitle">If you only memorize one section, memorize this one.</div></div></div>
<div class="mistake"><div class="mistake-num">MISTAKE 01</div><div class="mistake-title">Extremely short comments</div><div class="mistake-body"><em>"Not good." · "Weak candidate." · "Average."</em> — no justification, contradiction rises, the human decision can't be defended.</div></div>
<div class="mistake"><div class="mistake-num">MISTAKE 02</div><div class="mistake-title">Score–comment mismatch</div><div class="mistake-body">Score of 2 / 10 with the comment <em>"Good understanding overall."</em> Internal inconsistency is the fastest way to escalate contradiction severity.</div></div>
<div class="mistake"><div class="mistake-num">MISTAKE 03</div><div class="mistake-title">Reject without reasoning</div><div class="mistake-body">A bare <em>"Reject."</em> gives the AI nothing to reconcile against, so strictness reads as unexplained.</div></div>
<div class="mistake"><div class="mistake-num">MISTAKE 04</div><div class="mistake-title">Positive comment with negative status</div><div class="mistake-body"><em>"Candidate performed well overall."</em> + <strong>Status: Reject</strong> creates direct contradiction that suppression rules cannot cover.</div></div>
<div class="mistake"><div class="mistake-num">MISTAKE 05</div><div class="mistake-title">Missing fundamentals discussion</div><div class="mistake-body">If the candidate failed fundamentals but you don't mention it, the AI treats your low score as unexplained. Always document weak basics, shallow depth, probing failure, or inability to explain.</div></div>
</section>
<section class="section" id="sec-language"><div class="section-header"><div class="section-icon">12</div><div class="section-meta"><div class="section-num">Language Library</div><div class="section-title">Drop-In Phrases the Agent Recognizes</div><div class="section-subtitle">Use these formulations directly. They're the cleanest signal the suppression rules look for.</div></div></div>
<div class="lang-grid"><div class="lang-card"><div class="lang-card-title">Fundamentals weakness</div><ul><li>weak conceptual understanding</li><li>shallow fundamentals</li><li>struggled in follow-up reasoning</li><li>unable to explain tradeoffs</li><li>lacked depth beyond surface-level answers</li></ul></div><div class="lang-card"><div class="lang-card-title">Debugging weakness</div><ul><li>debugging approach was inefficient</li><li>struggled identifying edge cases</li><li>unable to isolate root cause effectively</li></ul></div><div class="lang-card"><div class="lang-card-title">Communication weakness</div><ul><li>explanations lacked structure</li><li>reasoning clarity decreased during probing</li><li>communication was inconsistent under follow-up</li></ul></div><div class="lang-card"><div class="lang-card-title">Authenticity / proxy signals</div><ul><li>responses appeared externally assisted</li><li>answer flow lacked natural continuity</li><li>performance inconsistency raised independence concerns</li><li>struggled when probing deviated from prepared answers</li></ul></div><div class="lang-card" style="grid-column:1/-1"><div class="lang-card-title">Borderline / mixed performance</div><ul><li>partial understanding observed</li><li>mixed performance across topics</li><li>acceptable implementation but weak conceptual depth</li><li>inconsistent reasoning quality</li></ul></div></div>
</section>
<section class="section" id="sec-checklist"><div class="section-header"><div class="section-icon">13</div><div class="section-meta"><div class="section-num">Pre-Submit Checklist</div><div class="section-title">Run This Before Submitting Every Evaluation</div><div class="section-subtitle">Tick every box. If they all pass, your contradiction risk is very low — regardless of how strict your scoring is.</div></div></div>
<div class="checklist-section" id="checklist"></div>
</section>
<div class="checklist-save-host">
  <div class="checklist-save-bar">
    <button type="button" id="btnSaveContradictionChecklist" class="checklist-save-btn" onclick="saveContradictionChecklistProgress()">
      <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M19 21H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h11l5 5v11a2 2 0 0 1-2 2z"></path><polyline points="17 21 17 13 7 13 7 21"></polyline><polyline points="7 3 7 8 15 8"></polyline></svg>
      Save checklist
    </button>
    <span id="checklistSaveStatus" class="checklist-save-status"></span>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway"><div class="takeaway-eyebrow">Final takeaway</div><div class="takeaway-title">The contradiction engine doesn't punish strictness — it punishes silence.</div><div class="takeaway-body">Stricter than the AI? Allowed. Rejecting a partially-correct candidate? Allowed. Disagreeing across every parameter? Still allowed — when you say <em style="font-style:normal;color:var(--white)">why</em>. Structured, justified, internally consistent evaluation is the entire job. When your comment names the weak fundamentals, shallow reasoning, debugging gaps, authenticity concerns, or inconsistent problem-solving you observed, the system reclassifies the difference automatically.</div><div class="takeaway-row"><div class="takeaway-col valid"><div class="takeaway-col-label">→ Valid Disagreement</div><div class="takeaway-col-text">Documented reasoning · weaknesses named · score, status, comment aligned · suppression rules engaged.</div></div><div class="takeaway-col contra"><div class="takeaway-col-label">→ True Contradiction</div><div class="takeaway-col-text">Vague comments · unsupported harsh scoring · status–comment mismatch · no reasoning behind the call.</div></div></div></div>
</main>
